1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,500
Your health is not fragmented. Your health data is.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:15,000
PHIL asks what changes when health technology starts with the whole person, not simply the easiest data point.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,500
Meet Maya, a fictional patient preparing for a follow-up visit.

4
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:30,000
PHIL brings her story, records, routines, practical limits, and goals into one patient-controlled view.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,500
The timeline places events in sequence while keeping every source and uncertainty visible.

6
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:45,000
Timing can guide a better question. It cannot establish cause, diagnosis, or treatment.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,500
Maya chooses the questions that belong in her Visit Preparation Brief.

8
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:00,000
Her priorities, budget, and capacity remain part of the story because feasibility matters.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,500
Before the brief can be shared, a human verifies identity, sources, patient intent, and uncertainty.

10
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:15,000
Incomplete review blocks approval. Clinical judgment is not delegated.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,500
PHIL does not diagnose or prescribe. It organizes, preserves provenance, and prepares the conversation.

12
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:30,000
The patient owns the story. The professional owns clinical judgment. Capability can be automated. Accountability cannot.
